---
title: Tuscan Beef Chuck Roast Ragù (Ragù di Manzo alla Toscana)
yield: 10 Portions (~2.5 kg finished ragù)
prep time: 45 minutes
cook time: 4.5 to 5 hours
---

= Rendering & Soffritto Base

Set a heavy-bottomed cast-iron #Dutch oven{} or large #rondeau{} over medium-low heat. Add the diced @pancetta{200%g} and @extra virgin olive oil{15%ml}. Slowly render the fat until the pancetta is lightly crisp and translucent (~{8-10%minutes}).

Add the ultra-fine brunoised @yellow onions{300%g}, @carrots{200%g}, and @celery{200%g}. Sweat gently over low heat for ~{20-25%minutes}.

> Chef’s Note: Do not brown or crisp the vegetables. You want a pale, translucent caramelization that accentuates natural sweetness without bitter, roasted notes.

Stir in the microplaned @garlic{5%cloves} during the final ~{3%minutes} of sweating. Remove the soffritto and pancetta mixture from the pan using a #slotted spoon{} and set aside in a warm #bowl{}.

= Hard Sear on the Beef Chuck Roast

Season the @beef chuck roast{1.8-2.0%kg}(trimmed of hard exterior fat, cut into ~6–8 large braising blocks) generously with @fine sea salt{} and @freshly cracked black pepper{}.

Increase pan heat to high and add the remaining @extra virgin olive oil{15%ml}. Working in batches to avoid crowding, sear the beef blocks heavily until a dark brown crust develops on all sides (~{3-4%minutes} per side).

Remove the seared beef blocks and set them aside with any accumulated juices. Add the @fresh pork belly{250%g}(skin removed, diced into small cubes) to the hot pan and brown quickly for ~{3-4%minutes}.

= Tomato Paste Caramelization & Early Milk Reduction

Turn the heat down to medium. Add the @tomato paste{100%g} directly to the hot fat in the pan.

Stir constantly for ~{3-4%minutes}, roasting the paste until it shifts from bright red to a deep reddish-brown mahogany.

Lower the heat and pour in 200 ml of the @whole milk{300%ml}. Stir into the caramelized tomato paste and scrape up early pan fond. Allow the milk to simmer down for ~{3-4%minutes}.

> Technique (Massimo Bottura): The lactic acid and enzymes in early milk addition help soften meat fibers and round out harsh tomato acidity early in the cook.

= Deglazing & Liquid Assembly

Pour in the @dry Tuscan red wine{400%ml}. Increase heat to medium-high and reduce by 50%, scraping every bit of caramelized fond off the bottom of the pan until clean.

Return the seared beef chuck, pork belly, and reserved soffritto to the pot.

Add the @tomato passata{700%g}, @rich beef stock{800%ml}, @Parmigiano-Reggiano rind{1%large}(~50–60 g, scraped clean), @fresh bay leaves{3}, @fresh rosemary{2%sprigs}(tied with twine), and @freshly grated nutmeg{1/2%tsp}. Stir thoroughly to combine. Bring to a gentle simmer.

= Low & Slow Oven Braise

Cover the Dutch oven with a tight-fitting #lid{} (or parchment cartouche + foil) and transfer to a preheated oven at 120°C.

Braise undisturbed for ~{4-4.5%hours}, until the beef chuck roast is ultra-tender and yields completely to a fork.

= Shredding & Sauce Finishing

Transfer the pot back to the stovetop. Carefully remove the cooked beef chuck blocks to a #tray{} using #tongs{} or a slotted spoon.

Discard the parmesan rind, bay leaves, and rosemary stems. Skim any excess surface oil if necessary.

Using two #forks{} or gloved hands, shred the beef chuck into medium-coarse strands. Return the shredded meat back into the rich sauce base.

Set the pan over low heat on the stovetop. Stir in the remaining 100 ml of whole milk.

> Technique (Marco Pierre White): Finishing with raw cold milk at the tail end binds the fat and liquid, eliminating harsh astringency and leaving a smooth texture.

Turn off the heat and mount the ragù with the cubed @cold unsalted butter{60%g}, stirring continuously until melted and fully emulsified into a glossy, velvety sauce. Adjust final seasoning with fine sea salt and freshly cracked black pepper.